up until recently north korea's leader  would never be seen in public wearing a  mask but now king jong-un can no longer  avoid it  local media is calling it an explosive  outbreak of covet 19.  dozens of people have died with more  than eight hundred thousand suspected  cases  the government has ordered a strict  lockdown but cases are spreading fast  on state television a doctor recommends  gargling with salt water if suffering  from a fever  with a crumbling health care system a  malnourished population and no vaccines  experts worry the pandemic could have a  devastating impact on north  korea the outbreak could lead to  shortages and an economic crisis in  order to prevent that the government's  conducting food rationing measures  but the surge in infections didn't stop  the government from testing its weapons  just hours after announcing the outbreak  on thursday it fired three ballistic  missiles off its east coast  this indicates that the country will  likely conduct missile tests more  frequently to counteract the depressed  atmosphere caused by covet 19 among  north korean citizens  the first cases of the omicron variant  were reported in april in its capital  pyongyang  the same month the city hosted several  large public celebrations where most  people did not wear masks  at the time cases were soaring in  neighboring china north korea's biggest  trade partner and close ally  we will pay close attention to the  developments of the epidemic in north  korea and maintain communication with  their government to protect the lives  and the health of our citizens who live  there  most of north korea's 26 million people  are not vaccinated the world health  organization offered inoculations last  year but kim jong-un rejected it  for now there's no sign people living in  the most isolated nation in the world  are closer to getting any help  katie lopez odan al jazeera
 

